description:
FOUND s497 (Fable, 2026-09-26) reviewing s494g (#2107), on f6a2348b; PRE-EXISTING on 3e8bff3e (the same answer), so not a merge blocker.  Probe `~/pcl-agent-scratch/s497/review/s494g/r9-pipe-ignore.pl`:

    $| = 1; $SIG{PIPE} = "IGNORE"; open(my $p, "|-", "true") or die; sleep 1;
    my $old = select($p); $| = 1; select($old);
    my $r = print $p "hello\n"; print "r=", ($r ? 1 : 0), " err=$!\n";
  perl:  r=0 err=Broken pipe        PCL: r=1 err=

`r9b-pipe-big.pl` (a 200 000-byte write) agrees with perl (`big r=0 err=Broken pipe`), and the default PIPE disposition kills both by signal 13 (`r9c-pipe-default.pl`), so #2107's EPIPE path IS wired -- what is missing is that `$|` on a piped-open handle does not make `print` WRITE at once: the data sits in a buffer, the write(2) that fails happens later.  Second half, same probe: after the failed write perl's `close($p)` answers TRUE (the child exited 0; the EPIPE was already reported by the print), PCL's answers FALSE.
WHERE: the piped-open handle's autoflush (`$|` / `IO::Handle::autoflush`) -- compare with how a FILE handle honours `$|` (fileio Pl/t rows); then the close return.  Perl-probed rows above are the guard.  Family: #2261 (qx STDERR), #2107.
